# Bridge process

## Linux (layout Pi)

The `lcos-bridge` systemd service runs `serial_to_mqtt.py` at boot and restarts it 10 s after any exit.
Log: `journalctl -u lcos-bridge` (or `./bridge log`). Control: `./bridge start | stop | restart | status | log`
(`scripts/linux/bridge.sh`); `./bridge flash` stops it, uploads the firmware, and starts it again. Port `/dev/lcos-nano`.

## Windows

The live bridge (`serial_to_mqtt.py`) is **operator-owned**. On the Windows mini PC it runs in the
**background**: the `LcosBridge` scheduled task starts `scripts/windows/bridge_runner.py` at boot (no login
needed). The runner timestamps all output into `logs/bridge.log` and restarts the bridge if it exits.
At logon, `start_bridge_tabs.cmd` (in shell:startup; source in `scripts/windows/`) opens a "Bridge log"
tab (`bridge log`, i.e. tail -f) and an "MQTT sub" tab.

Control: `bridge start | stop | restart | status | log` (`bridge.cmd` in the repo root ->
`scripts/windows/bridge.ps1`). `bridge install` / `bridge uninstall` (elevated) manage the task.

## Do

- Read `logs/bridge.log` (or `bridge status`) to see what the bridge is doing.
- For a lab test that needs the COM port: `bridge stop`, run the test, then **`bridge start`** before
  ending the turn, and confirm with `bridge status`.
- After firmware/agent changes that need a restart: **ask the user** to run `bridge restart` (or run it
  yourself if they asked you to).

## Do not

- Run a second bridge (`run_serial_mqtt.cmd`, `start_bridge_detached.py`, ...) while the background one
  is running: two processes would fight over the serial port. `bridge status` warns about a foreground bridge.
- Leave the bridge stopped after your work, or leave stray test bridges running.
